[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, you\'ve set up your local AI, it\'s running without a sweat, you even got an always-on rig you can hit from a coffee shop, but every time you start a new session, you\'re feeding all your important context back to it again and again. So, you think, "Fine, let me add'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a memory system." But, most of them either eat too many resources, or they\'re hosted in the cloud, or your local model just isn\'t sharp enough to call the right tool at the right moment. And no, RAG is not the answer here,'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'either. I\'ll get to why later. Here\'s the real problem. Most of these systems are built for frontier models, the ones that can handle tools even with half-baked instructions. Andrej Karpathy shared his LLM Wiki idea, and Google'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standardized something similar with OKF. But, asking a regular local model to follow that spec to the letter, while also doing the actual job you gave it, that\'s a huge ask. You\'re diluting its'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'intelligence across too many things at once. So, I tried to shift the memory burden off the AI and onto the tool itself. One dead-simple tool built on Karpathy\'s LLM Wiki and Google\'s OKF.'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And spoiler alert, it did not go as smoothly as I thought. So, did it work? What broke when I ran it on my own local setup? And can this really become the permanent memory for a local AI stack?'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s find out. Okay, so before we move any further, let\'s talk about why not just use RAG. RAG is good and efficient when you\'ve'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got a knowledge base that\'s almost fixed, GBs or TBs of data that barely changes, and you want fast answers. But, look at how it works. It uses semantic indexing to do similarity'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'matching. The intelligence of the LLM is never used during retrieval. It\'s just vector matching, and that\'s the trade-off you take to get fast retrieval out of huge data.'

[01:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But a text-based LLM wiki is different. It\'s like actual memory. It needs careful retrieval with linked memories and precise refinement leveraging the intelligence of the AI.'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It reads one file and from there it reads another and then another to precisely find the exact thing we need out of our memory. And we\'re not storing GBs of data here.'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We\'re storing personal context. The things the AI decides are important to remember for the future. And since that\'s constantly evolving and updating, we don\'t want to be'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 're-running, chunking, and embedding every single time the AI updates something. If you\'ve ever used a coding agent, you already know this works. Your coding agent browses the files to find exactly'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where a piece of code lives and how it links to the rest of the code base just by reading and following the breadcrumbs like a detective.'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'My first attempt was the obvious one, a skill file. It embeds the OKF spec plus instructions telling the AI where the root of the memory folder is and it didn\'t really work. I mean, it kind of'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'worked, but a lot of the time the AI wouldn\'t use the root path. It would create the folder in the wrong place instead and it would forget to update the index after creating a new file. It was a mess. The concept works fine when'

[03:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve got a Claude Opus-level model or higher, but a local LLM struggles to follow instructions with that kind of precision. So, does that mean it\'s over? That a local LLM just isn\'t good enough for'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this kind of job? Well, not really. We just need to get a little more creative to make it work. So, I needed a way to make this actually work. And there are a few ways to'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'magnify an AI\'s capability using sub-agents and harnesses. Because here\'s the thing, why burden your AI with all these extra instructions when it already has its own actual problems to solve?'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We\'ve already solved this in real life. We store human knowledge and history in libraries, and every library has, you guessed it, a librarian. And it\'s the librarian\'s job to take care of the'

[04:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'books, organize them, keep them maintained. If I want to know something, I just ask the librarian, and he goes and finds it for me. I have no idea how the library is organized, what index it'

[04:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uses, what registry, anything at all. And that\'s exactly what we are going to use here. So, the setup is simple, a library to store the memories and knowledge, and a librarian agent that takes a query, goes and finds it in the'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'library, and hands you the answer. And when new or updated knowledge comes in, the librarian organizes it the best way it can, so it can be found on the next query. Now, in technical terms, that'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'means a carefully organized memory, indexed at every level with an index.md, laid out so it\'s intuitive for the LLM to traverse, with links between the memory files, so when it\'s searching, it'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can refer to the right sections. And the system is deterministic. The rules are fixed, and it only uses the LLM when it actually needs to make a decision. That way, we never drift away from the spec.'

[05:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I built the whole system, and I added a web UI, so I could actually talk to the librarian agent directly, and I chatted with it for a while, watching the memory grow on the side as we'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'talked. I also added a graph visualizer that shows all the memories and how they link to each other. And here\'s the fun part, it also records the traces of every query the agent runs to find an answer. It\'s a neat way to actually see'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how the agent is finding things because if it\'s easy to visualize, it\'s easy to debug. Okay, but now I need my other AI systems to plug into this and use it as their'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'memory layer. And that comes down to a handful of MCP tools. Query. Here the librarian only has read tools. Any of my local systems that needs to look something up just asks the'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'librarian and the librarian finds it and answers. Update memory. Now the librarian gets read and edit. If the AI finds a memory that\'s outdated, it just asks the'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'librarian to update it. And add memory. Read, edit, and write. If the AI runs into something worth keeping, it calls add memory and the librarian either adds it to an existing file or creates a new'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one for it. And because the librarian handles all of this itself, I never have to bloat my actual agent\'s context with the OK of spec or a pile of memory instructions. And one more thing, the'

[06:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'librarian is an agent itself. So it needs a brain of its own, right? Well, it just connects to the same llama.cpp server that everything else in my stack is already using. It even auto discovers whatever model is currently loaded. It'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works behind llama swap too and it prefers the loaded model. So a memory query never triggers a model swap. So think about it. The harness, the memory, and the model, the whole loop,'

[07:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything is running on my own box. There\'s no cloud API hiding anywhere in the chain. This thing is truly local. Real quick, before we get into the challenges, if you\'re into building'

[07:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'local AI, this channel is for you. I figure this stuff out in the open so you don\'t have to. Subscribe and let\'s take back control together. Okay, back to it. So, on paper it was done. Then I plugged'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the real model into it over MCP, and that\'s exactly where it fell apart. It had a kind of amnesia it didn\'t even know it had. All the client model could see was the tool names, query, add, update status, that\'s it. So, when you'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'asked it something, it just answered from its own head. It never thought to check the memory first because it had no idea the memory even had anything useful in it. The knowledge was sitting right there on disk, but the model couldn\'t'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see it, so it never looked. And here\'s the ironic part. The internal agent was totally fine. We\'d been feeding it an overview of the knowledge base in its system prompt the whole time. It was the client model out on the other end of the'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wire that was flying blind. So, the fix was to give it a reason to look first. The moment a session connects, we generate a seed memory, a small live summary of what\'s actually in'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the knowledge base, and we push it through the two channels that reach the client model at startup. The MCP instructions field and the description of the query tool. Now, the model opens every session already knowing, "Okay,'

[08:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s a segment about billing, one about people, one about infrastructure." And that\'s enough. It stops guessing and starts querying. We tightened it even more later. Instead of listing file names, the seed lists'

[09:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what each concept is about because your question is way more likely to run into a 100 requests per minute per client than into a file called rate limits.md. Once it was reliably writing to memory,'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a subtler problem showed up. It treated memory like a junk drawer. Every new fact became its own file. I tell it, "I live in Hyderabad." And it would create a standalone Hyderabad.md'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just floating there, disconnected from the concept about me that already existed. So, the memory kept growing, but none of it was connected. It was a pile of notes, not actual knowledge. A person taking notes would never do that. They\'d'

[09:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just add lives in Hyderabad to your existing profile. So, I gave it two rules. First, enrich before you create. Before writing anything, search for the concept this fact belongs to. If it\'s just an'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'attribute of something that already exist, patch it into that. Don\'t spawn a new file. Second, link both ways. When it genuinely is a new thing, say a YouTube channel called Kodikes, create that'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'concept, but then go back and patch the related ones, too. So, now my profile says runs the Kodikes channel, and the channel says created by Anirban. Now, one honest note on the model. I'

[10:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first tried this with Gemma, the 12B. On the mechanical side, it was fine, but it just wasn\'t strong enough on the agentic side, the actual decisions about how to organize the memory, what to enrich,'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what to link. It kept getting those wrong. So, I went back to my trusty Qwen 3.6, the 35B, A3B. It never fails me. And I watched that model running on a'

[10:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single consumer GPU do exactly this on its own. Enrich the small facts, wire up the big ones. No hand-holding. By now, the memory was behaving itself,'

[11:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but I had a feeling I was reinventing a wheel someone smarter had already drawn. So, I went back to what Karpathy actually laid out in his LLM Wiki idea. And there were a few things I was still'

[11:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'missing. First, graph health. I added a lint step, completely deterministic, that walks all the links between concepts and flags two things. Orphans, concepts nothing links to, and'

[11:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'broken links that point nowhere. One catch that really mattered. The auto-generated index files had to be excluded as link sources. Otherwise, everything looks connected and orphan'

[11:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detection is useless. Then a maintain tool that adds on that. The lint finds the orphans and an internal agent wires them back into the concepts they relate to both directions.'

[11:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I ran it on my local Quine. It found an orphan playbook and patched both related concepts to point at it. Orphans went from one to zero on its own.'

[12:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the last one, contradictions. If I told it I moved from Hyderabad to Bangalore, the old version would just append Bangalore and leave Hyderabad sitting in the file. So now the concept claims both cities.'

[12:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The fix was to make the rule mechanical. The old fact has to be gone everywhere. Rewrite the whole thing if you have to. After that, it cleanly said Bangalore'

[12:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and only Bangalore. And here\'s the part that actually made it really amazing. Because it\'s all going through the MCP layer, every AI agent I use can share the same memory.'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So whichever tool I\'m in, it already knows what happened in the other one. It\'s fully personalized. It knows what\'s going on in my system, what tool I prefer, what the IP of my local box is without me reminding it every session.'

[12:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, did it work? Yeah, it became the memory of my AI. It\'s the thing I use now every day. Every tool I open already knows my projects, my contacts, what I\'ve been'

[13:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'working on. I don\'t reintroduce myself anymore. But I\'ll be honest with you, this isn\'t finished. And that\'s actually my favorite part. It\'s a living thing and I\'m going to keep building on it long'

[13:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'after this video goes up. Like the next thing I\'m adding is a cache layer, a hot tier of your most recent memories that the agent always keeps close, so it never has to go digging through the'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whole library just to remember what happened 5 minutes ago. Recent stuff, instantly there. And after that, this is the one I\'m actually excited about, a dreaming feature. At certain points in the day when it\'s idle, the memory goes off on'

[13:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its own and reorganizes itself. Cleans things up, links what should be linked, the way your brain consolidates everything while you sleep. So, it\'s not just storing what you tell'

[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it, it\'s getting better at remembering while you\'re not even using it. And there\'s more after that. Because here\'s what I keep coming back to. A model on your own machine is already great. Private, yours, nothing leaves the box.'

[14:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But without memory, it\'s still a stranger. The moment you give it a memory that follows you around, one that lives on your disk as plain markdown you can open and read yourself, it stops being a'

[14:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generic chatbot and starts being your personal AI. One that knows you across different harnesses. And that\'s what personal AI was always supposed to mean. Running it locally is only half of it.'

[14:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The other half is owning what it knows about you. We took back the model, the hardware, the privacy. Memory is the next thing to take back, and now it\'s just sitting there in your control. The whole'

[14:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'project\'s open, built on Karpathy\'s LLM wiki and Google\'s OKF spec, links in the description. Clone it, plug it into your own setup, give your local AI a memory. And keep an eye on it because it\'s only'

[15:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to get better. If you build something on top of it or there\'s something you want to see in it, tell me in the comments. I\'ll see you on the next one.'
